--- title Making a set of characters
--- about A bitset says which characters are in it, which is what a parse
--- about rule needs to mean "any of these" without writing them as
--- about alternatives. TO BITSET! builds one from whatever names the
--- about characters, and CHARSET is the name most code uses.

--- id bitsets/a-set-from-a-string
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Each character of the string is a member.
--- code
mold to bitset! "abc"
--- result
"#(bitset! #{00000000000000000000000070})"

--- id bitsets/a-set-from-a-block-of-characters
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
mold to bitset! [#"a" #"b"]
--- result
"#(bitset! #{00000000000000000000000060})"

--- id bitsets/a-set-from-a-block-of-code-points
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note Numbers name characters by their code point, which is how a
--- note set of control characters is written without spelling them.
--- code
mold to bitset! [1 2 3]
--- result
"#(bitset! #{70})"

--- id bitsets/a-set-from-a-range
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note A dash between two characters means everything between them,
--- note which is the only way to write a large set at all.
--- code
b: to bitset! [#"a" - #"c"]
find b #"b"
--- result
#(true)

--- id bitsets/asking-whether-a-character-is-in-a-set
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note FIND answers whether rather than where, because a bitset has
--- note no order and no position.
--- code
b: charset "abc"
find b #"a"
--- result
#(true)

--- id bitsets/a-character-that-is-not-in-the-set
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: charset "abc"
find b #"z"
--- result
#(false)

--- id bitsets/two-sets-joined
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note The set operations work on bitsets as well as on blocks, which
--- note is how a rule builds a character class out of named pieces
--- note rather than spelling every member.
--- code
a: charset "ab"
b: charset "bc"
find union a b #"c"
--- result
#(true)

--- id bitsets/only-what-two-sets-share
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
a: charset "ab"
b: charset "bc"
find intersect a b #"a"
--- result
#(false)

--- id bitsets/what-the-first-has-and-the-second-does-not
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
a: charset "ab"
b: charset "bc"
find exclude a b #"a"
--- result
#(true)

--- id bitsets/everything-the-set-does-not-hold
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note COMPLEMENT of a set is the set of everything else, which is
--- note what a rule means by "any character but these".
--- code
a: charset "ab"
find complement a #"a"
--- result
#(false)

--- id bitsets/adding-a-character-to-a-set
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- note APPEND on a bitset adds members rather than putting something
--- note at the end, because a bitset has no end to put anything at.
--- code
b: charset "a"
append b #"b"
find b #"b"
--- result
#(true)

--- id bitsets/what-was-already-in-the-set-stays
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
b: charset "a"
append b #"b"
find b #"a"
--- result
#(true)

--- id bitsets/adding-a-whole-string-of-members
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1
--- code
c: charset "a"
append c "xy"
find c #"x"
--- result
#(true)
